Data Collection Tools T1 LIFE BEFORE AND AFTER DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION -Temporal Contrast and Lived Experience Administration Instructions: This opening theme uses memory and temporal comparison as an accessible entry point. Do not introduce the word ‘digital’ initially -let participants frame the change in their own words. Warm-up gently before moving to specific digital barriers. Opening Question: (avoid repetition- carefully review SQ-OOP) ‘Let us begin from the beginning. Could you tell me a little about yourself -how long you have lived in this city, and what daily life was like for you here about ten to fifteen years ago? What did a normal week look like for you back then?’ Probing Questions / Follow-ups:

Where did you go to pay your bills, visit a doctor, or collect your pension in those days?
Were those places easy for you to reach? Did you go alone or with someone?
Was there a particular public place -a park, a market, a religious place -that you visited regularly? What was that experience like?
What were the things you could do completely independently in those days that gave you a sense of confidence or pride? Transition Question: (avoid reptition- carefully review SQ-OOP) ‘Now, comparing that to today -what does your typical week look like now? What has changed about how you go about your daily tasks in this city?’ Probing Questions / Follow-ups:
Has your neighbourhood or the physical city changed in ways that affect how you move around or access services?
Are there places you used to go regularly that you no longer visit? Why is that?
What has become easier in the city over the years, and what has become harder for you personally?
[If participant does not mention digital barriers organically]: 'Have you noticed that many services have moved to mobile phones, apps, or online systems? How has that affected you?’ Field Investigator Note: Let the participant narrate freely in this theme. Do NOT impose the word ‘digital’. Your goal is to understand their lived experience of the city and then observe whether digital systems emerge as a significant barrier from their own unprompted narration. Note in the post-interview log whether digital barriers were raised spontaneously. ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 20



Data Collection Tools T2 SPECIFIC EXPERIENCES WITH SMART CITY SERVICES -Incident-Based Narration Administration Instructions: Reference the city-specific SCM service card here. Tailor the service mentioned in the main question to the most prominent SCM service in that city: Delhi Metro app or Mohalla Clinic booking for Delhi; iNagar app for Gandhinagar; 311 app or BRTS for Indore; smart park entry for Chennai; smart bus shelters for Srinagar; ICCC civic services for Aizawl. Main Question: ‘I would like to hear about specific experiences you have had with city services here. Have you ever tried to use or access [city-specific SCM service -name it clearly]? Can you tell me exactly what happened? Walk me through it step by step -from the beginning’. Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 9. Where were you exactly when you tried to use it? Were you alone, or was someone with you? 10. What was the first thing you saw or were asked to do? What happened next? 11. At what point did you find it difficult, if you did? What specifically was the difficulty? 12. How did you feel at that exact moment? 13. What did you do after you ran into the difficulty? Did you eventually manage to complete what you needed? 14. Did you have to ask someone for help? Who was it? How did that interaction feel? 15. Have you tried to use that service again after that experience? Why or why not? Follow-up Question: Can you recall another incident -perhaps with a different service, a hospital appointment, a government office, a bus or metro station, or a park entry -where digital systems made things particularly difficult for you? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 16. Was there a specific moment when you felt very strongly that ‘this system is not meant for people like me’? 17. Have you ever been turned away from a service entirely because you did not have the required digital access? 18. Have you ever had to pay a middleman or agent to do something that should have been free for you? 19. [Srinagar-specific probe]: ‘When internet connectivity is disrupted for days, how does that specifically affect your access to city services?’ 20. [Aizawl-specific probe]: ‘Does your church community or the local council (Mualchin) help older members navigate digital city services? Can you tell me more?’ 21. [Chennai-specific probe]: ‘Has anyone from a women’s SHG or community group helped you navigate online services? What was that like?’ ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 21



Data Collection Tools T3 EMOTIONAL EXPERIENCE, DIGITAL ANXIETY, AND MENTAL WELL-BEING Administration Instructions: This theme probes sensitive emotional territory. Move slowly. Allow silences -do NOT fill them quickly. If the participant becomes visibly distressed, pause and offer a break. If distress continues, end this theme gently and move to Theme 4, or terminate the session if necessary. Note any distress in the post-interview log and inform PI. Main Question: ‘I would like to ask you now about how all of this makes you feel -emotionally. How do you feel when you are unable to complete something that your children, grandchildren, or younger neighbours can easily do on their phones?’ Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 22. Can you describe that feeling in more detail -is it frustration? Sadness? Embarrassment? Helplessness? Something else? 23. Is there a particular incident that stands out in your memory where you felt especially distressed because of a digital barrier? 24. Have you ever felt afraid of making a serious mistake -like losing money -while using a device for something important? 25. Do you ever feel that you are somehow ‘too old’ for this city now? What does that mean to you when you feel that? 26. Has any of this affected your sleep, your appetite, or your overall mood or emotional state? Follow-up on Independence and Dignity: ‘Some older people we have spoken with told us that they now feel they need permission or help from family members to do things they used to do on their own. Is that true for you? How does that feel?’ Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 27. Do you feel like a burden to your family or caregivers when you need help with digital tasks? 28. Has your sense of independence changed over recent years? In what way, and what has caused that change in your view? 29. How do you cope emotionally when something goes wrong digitally -do you have any way of managing that feeling? 30. Some people told us they have started avoiding going to certain places because of digital barriers. Has that happened to you? Tell me about that. T4 SOCIAL CONNECTIONS, FAMILY DYNAMICS, AND INTERGENERATIONAL BONDS Main Question: ‘Let us talk about your relationships -with your family, your neighbours, and other people around you. Who do you usually turn to when you need help with something digital? How often does that happen?’ Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 31. How does that person typically respond when you ask for help -are they patient, impatient, or indifferent? 32. Have there been times when you felt reluctant to ask for help again from the same person? Why? 33. Do you feel the technology gap between you and your younger family members has changed the way they interact with you, or how they see you? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 22



Data Collection Tools 34. Are there other older people your age in your neighbourhood or old age home who face similar difficulties? Do you discuss this among yourselves? 35. Do younger neighbours, volunteers, or community members ever help older people in your area with digital tasks? City-Specific Community Probes: [Aizawl only] ‘In Mizoram, community bonds through the church and YMA are known to be very strong. Does your church community or local council actively help older members navigate city services, including digital ones?’ [Srinagar only] ‘Do mohalla committees or mosque-based community structures in your area help older residents access government services?’ [New Delhi only] ‘This city has a very large population of elderly migrants from other states. Do you feel connected to any community network from your place of origin that helps you here?’ T5 RIGHTS AWARENESS, DIGNITY, AND POLICY -Constitutional and Legal Consciousness Main Question: ‘Do you feel that as an older citizen -someone who has contributed to this country and this city -you have the right to access all city services in a way that works for you, including digital services?’ Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 36. Do you think the government has a responsibility to make sure that city services are accessible to people who cannot use digital devices? 37. Have you ever made a formal complaint about being excluded from a service because of a digital barrier? To whom? What happened? 38. Are you aware of any law or government policy that specifically protects the rights and welfare of older persons in India? 39. Has any government official, social worker, or NGO representative ever explained your rights to you as an older person? Legal Awareness Probes [Use only if participant appears aware of legal framework]: Have you heard of the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 40. Do you know what rights that law gives you? 41. Do you think that law should be updated to also protect older people from being excluded by digital city infrastructure and services? Closing Question: If you could sit with the people who design digital systems for this city -the apps, the kiosks, the online portals -and give them one clear message from your own experience, what would it be? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 42. What would you want them to truly understand about your daily life? 43. What one change would make this city work better for you? 44. Is there anything else -anything at all -that you want to share with us that we have not yet asked you about? [FI closing protocol:] ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 23



