Data Collection Tools TOOL 4: INTERVIEW GUIDE -OLD AGE HOME AND CARE INSTITUTION ADMINISTRATORS (SSI-OAH-Admin-v1.0) Instrument Code SSI-OAH-Admin-v1.0 Instrument Title Semi-Structured Interview -Old Age Home and Elderly Care Institution Administrators Type Qualitative -Semi-Structured Institutional Interview with Institutional Profile Data Collection Target Stakeholder OAH Directors, Wardens, Programme Coordinators; NGO-run elderly care centre heads; government-run Vridh Ashram managers Applicable Cities All six cities -minimum 2 institutions per city (at least one government + one private/NGO) Session Duration 60–75 minutes Administration Mode Audio-recorded with consent. English or Hindi with local language support. Administration Instructions: Request these documents IN ADVANCE -at least 1 week before the interview:[ ] Resident age and gender distribution data (anonymized)[ ] Any programme schedule for digital literacy or digital assistance activities[ ] List of government schemes residents are currently enrolled in[ ] Any SOPs for helping residents access online government servicesReview documents before interview to formulate targeted probes. Institutional Profile Data Collection Template Complete this table from documents and administrator responses before/during interview: Institution Name (to be anonymised in publications) Type: Government / Private / NGO-run / Public-Private Year Established Total Number of Residents Residents aged 75–85 (number and percentage) Residents aged 85 and above (number) Gender distribution (Male : Female ratio) Educational background of residents -approximate breakdown Government schemes residents are enrolled in (list) ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 30



Data Collection Tools Wi-Fi or internet available on premises? (Yes/No/Limited) Digital devices available for resident use? (Yes/No -specify) Digital literacy programme currently offered? (Yes/No -if Yes, specify provider) Dedicated staff for digital assistance to residents? (Yes/No) Has the institution faced any digital compliance requirements (biometric, online forms)? T1 RESIDENT PROFILE AND DIGITAL CAPABILITY BASELINE Could you tell me about your residents above 75 years -their background, their prior experience with technology before coming here, and their current digital capabilities? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 89. What proportion of your residents above 75 years have had any prior exposure to digital devices -computers, mobile phones -in their working or personal lives? 90. Do you have any residents in this age group who actively and independently use a smartphone or digital services? What proportion would you estimate? 91. Are residents’ cognitive abilities considered when designing any digital activities or assistance programmes? 92. What are the most common physical or cognitive barriers you observe among your oldest residents that affect their ability to interact with digital devices? T2 DIGITAL ACCESS TO CITY SERVICES AND SMART CITY INTERACTION Let me ask about how your residents access city services -particularly the services that are now primarily digital or online. Does this institution facilitate that access in any formal way? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 93. Are residents able to access their pensions, government benefits, or health services through online systems? Who helps them with this, and how systematically is that support provided? 94. Has any resident in this institution ever missed a government benefit payment or welfare entitlement because they could not complete the required online process? How frequently does this occur? 95. Are there any specific Smart City services in [city name] that your residents have tried to use or have needed to use? What has been their experience with those services? 96. Does the institution itself require residents to interact with digital systems -for example, biometric attendance, online registration for government inspections, e-health portals? Do residents find these difficult or distressing? 97. What is the most common digital barrier you personally observe or hear about among your oldest residents on a day-to-day basis? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 31



Data Collection Tools T3 EMOTIONAL AND PSYCHOLOGICAL IMPACT ON RESIDENTS From your daily interaction with and observation of residents, have you noticed any emotional or psychological effects of digital exclusion or digital frustration on residents above 75? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 98. Have residents ever expressed frustration, shame, sadness, or distress specifically about their inability to use digital systems? 99. Have you observed any residents withdrawing from group activities, social interaction, or general engagement related to their difficulties with technology? 100. In your professional observation, do you think digital exclusion in the wider city contributes to isolation, loss of agency, or cognitive decline among your oldest residents? 101. Are there residents who have become noticeably more dependent on institution staff or on family for basic city service access in recent years? Is digital exclusion a contributing factor in your view? T4 INSTITUTIONAL NEEDS, RECOMMENDATIONS, AND POLICY PERSPECTIVES Finally, based on your experience running this institution and your deep knowledge of your residents -what would be most helpful for genuinely improving digital access and digital well-being for your oldest residents? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 102. What specific resources -from government, NGOs, or technology providers -would most meaningfully help your institution improve digital access for residents above 75? 103. Do you believe current Indian law and policy on senior citizens adequately protects your residents from digital exclusion in public spaces and city services? What is missing? 104. Have you ever engaged directly with your city’s Smart City SPV or municipal corporation on the issue of digital accessibility for your residents? If yes, what was the outcome? 105. If you could recommend one specific legislative or policy change to address digital exclusion for oldest old residents in institutional care, what would it be? 106. Are you aware of any other institution in India or internationally that you think does an exemplary job of facilitating digital access for the oldest old? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 32



Data Collection Tools TOOL 5: INTERVIEW GUIDE -DIGITAL LITERACY PROGRAMME TRAINERS AND COORDINATORS (SSI-DLT-v1.0) Instrument Code SSI-DLT-v1.0 Instrument Title Semi-Structured Interview -Digital Literacy Programme Trainers and Programme Coordinators Type Qualitative -Semi-Structured Interview with Programme Data Collection Target Stakeholder Trainers and coordinators of government-run (CSC, PM-WANI, etc.) or NGO/private digital literacy programmes targeting elderly; ISRO/NIC digital awareness programme staff Applicable Cities All six cities -locate programmes through district CSC offices, state IT departments, HelpAge India, and local NGOs Session Duration 45–60 minutes Administration Mode Audio-recorded with consent. Programme Data Collection Template (complete from records before or during interview) Programme Name and Funding Source Year the programme started Cities / areas currently covered Total participants enrolled (all-time estimate) Participants aged 60 and above (approximate %) Participants aged 75 and above (approximate % -if tracked) Total programme duration per participant (hours) Trainer-to-participant ratio per session Completion rate (% who finish the full programme) Language(s) of instruction Format: Classroom / Mobile / Home-visit / Online / Mixed Government services taught in curriculum (list key ones) SCM-specific services explicitly taught (list) ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 33



Data Collection Tools Formal evaluation or impact assessment conducted? Y/N If Yes: by whom and key findings T1 PROGRAMME DESIGN, CURRICULUM, AND CONTENT Opening: Tell me about the digital literacy programme you run -who designed it, when was it developed, and what is its stated primary goal? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 107. Was this programme specifically designed for elderly participants, or is it a general digital literacy programme that has been adapted for them? 108. What specific digital skills or tasks does the curriculum teach? Does it cover the specific SCM services available in this city -the apps, the kiosks, the online portals? 109. Are the training materials available in the local language of this area? Are they designed for participants with low literacy? 110. Does the curriculum account for the cognitive and physical limitations common in the 75-85 age group -slower processing speed, tremors, vision issues? 111. How long is the programme, how many sessions, and what is the format? Has the format been changed based on participant feedback? T2 BARRIERS TO LEARNING AMONG OLDEST OLD PARTICIPANTS Now I would like to understand what it is actually like to train participants above 75 years old. What have you observed in your sessions? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 112. What are the most common and persistent barriers you encounter when training participants above 75 years? 113. Which specific tasks or interface elements do oldest old participants find most difficult? For example: entering OTPs, touchscreen navigation, CAPTCHA systems, small text, multiple steps? 114. Do participants in this age group learn differently from younger seniors in terms of pace, retention, or confidence? In what specific ways? 115. Have you adapted your training methods specifically for cognitive or physical limitations in the 75+ group? What adaptations have been most effective? 116. Do participants become anxious or distressed during training sessions? What typically triggers that, and how do you handle it? 117. What is your dropout rate specifically for participants above 75, and what are the main reasons they discontinue? T3 PROGRAMME OUTCOMES AND REAL-WORLD USABILITY ASSESSMENT After participants above 75 complete your programme, are they actually able to independently access the digital services that matter to them in their daily lives in this city? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 118. What is your programme’s ‘success’ definition and metric? How do you measure or assess whether the training has actually worked? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 34



Data Collection Tools 119. Do you have any follow-up data from 3 or 6 months after training on whether participants retain the skills they learned and continue using them? 120. In your honest professional assessment -not just the official numbers -can participants above 75 independently use the key Smart City services in [city] after completing your programme? 121. Do you think the digital interfaces of SCM services in this city are actually compatible with the skill level your participants realistically achieve at the end of your programme? 122. Can you share a specific example of a participant above 75 who successfully and independently used a smart city service after your training? What made that possible? 123. Conversely, can you share a specific example of a participant who completed training but still cannot navigate city services independently? What were the barriers? T4 RECOMMENDATIONS FOR IDEAL PROGRAMME DESIGN AND SYSTEM CHANGE Based on everything you have learned from your direct experience, what would a genuinely effective digital literacy programme specifically for people between 75 and 85 years old in this city look like? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 124. What content would it include? What would it deliberately exclude or defer? 125. What format, duration, and delivery method would work best for this age group? 126. Who should deliver it -government trainers, NGO workers, youth volunteers, intergenerational family mentors? 127. Critically -what would need to change in the CITY’s own digital infrastructure for your programme's outcomes to result in genuine independent usage by this age group? 128. What single policy change or funding mechanism would most strengthen the work you are doing? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 35



Data Collection Tools TOOL 6: INTERVIEW GUIDES -NGOs, GERIATRICIANS, AND LEGAL / CONSTITUTIONAL EXPERTS TOOL 6A: Interview Guide -NGOs Working with Elderly (SSI-NGO-v1.0) Instrument Code SSI-NGO-v1.0 Instrument Title Semi-Structured Interview -NGOs and Civil Society Organisations Working with Elderly Type Qualitative -Semi-Structured Interview Target Stakeholder HelpAge India, Agewell Foundation, ARDSI, Dignity Foundation, and local city-level NGOs working with elderly Applicable Cities All six cities -city-specific NGO contacts per city (see city gatekeeper lists) Session Duration 50–65 minutes Administration Mode Audio-recorded with consent. Opening: Tell me about your organisation's work with elderly residents in [city] -the services you provide, who your primary beneficiaries are, and how long you have been working in this space. Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 129. In your field experience and case documentation, how have older residents -particularly those above 75 -been affected by the increasing digitalization of city services over the past five years? 130. Has your organisation documented specific cases where oldest old persons were denied a service, a welfare benefit, or access to a public space because of a digital barrier? Can you describe some of those cases without identifying individuals? 131. Does your organisation actively help elderly people navigate digital city services? In what specific ways, and with what resources? 132. What are the most common complaints, distress situations, or cases of abandonment (giving up on a service) that you see among oldest old persons related to digital city services? 133. Do you observe significant differences in how different groups of oldest old experience digital exclusion -for example, women versus men, migrants versus long-term residents, literate versus illiterate, those with family support versus those without? 134. Has your organisation engaged formally with the city’s Smart City SPV or municipal corporation on the issue of elderly digital inclusion? What was the result of that engagement? 135. What advocacy work has your organisation undertaken, or are you planning, specifically related to digital inclusion for oldest old persons in smart cities? 136. What do you believe is most critically missing from current law and policy -at the central or state level -that would meaningfully protect oldest old persons from digital exclusion in public urban spaces? 137. What data, case records, or evidence does your organisation hold on this issue that you would be willing to share formally with this research? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 36



Data Collection Tools TOOL 6B: Expert Interview -Geriatricians and Mental Health Professionals (SEI-Geri-v1.0) Instrument Code SEI-Geri-v1.0 Instrument Title Structured Expert Interview -Geriatricians, Gerontologists, and Elderly Mental Health Specialists Type Qualitative -Expert Interview with Clinical Expertise Focus Target Stakeholder Geriatricians; Gerontologists; Psychiatrists or psychologists specializing in elderly; Neurologists with geriatric practice Applicable Cities Primarily Delhi and Chennai (AIIMS affiliates, specialist hospitals); accessible from any city Session Duration 45–60 minutes Administration Mode Audio-recorded with consent. Opening: Your expertise is in [geriatrics/gerontology/elderly mental health]. I would like to understand, from a clinical and research perspective, how digital systems and technology-dense environments affect the cognitive, emotional, and physical well-being of persons aged between 75 and 85 years. Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 138. What are the primary cognitive changes occurring in persons aged 75-85 that would affect their ability to successfully interact with modern digital interfaces -touchscreens, apps, kiosks, QR codes? 139. Is what we might call 'digital anxiety' -psycho-emotional stress induced by anticipated or actual failure in a digital interaction -a phenomenon you observe or recognise clinically among your oldest patients? What are its typical manifestations? 140. Do you have clinical observations or documented cases of patients in the 75-85 age group who have experienced measurable deterioration in psychological well-being -depression, social withdrawal, or loss of confidence and agency -that is linked to their inability to navigate digital public services? 141. Are there documented clinical studies or published evidence on the cognitive load imposed by modern digital interfaces on persons above 75? What does the available evidence indicate? 142. What specific design features in digital interfaces -whether kiosk, mobile app, or web portal -are most cognitively and motorically harmful or exclusionary for the oldest old from a clinical standpoint? 143. Conversely, what specific design features or interface characteristics would make digital interactions genuinely usable and cognitively safe for your oldest patients? 144. Is there clinical or epidemiological evidence to support the argument that digital exclusion in public urban spaces -being functionally locked out of city services -contributes to or accelerates cognitive decline, depression, or social isolation in the oldest old? 145. From a public health and geriatric medicine standpoint, what specific recommendations would you make to policymakers designing digital city infrastructure to prevent harm to the oldest old? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 37



Data Collection Tools TOOL 6C: Expert Interview -Constitutional Law and Elder Rights Legal Scholars (EI-Legal-Ac-v1.0) Instrument Code EI-Legal-Ac-v1.0 Instrument Title Expert Interview -Constitutional Law Academics, Elder Rights Advocates, and Technology Law Specialists Type Qualitative -Expert Interview with Constitutional and Legal Analysis Focus Target Stakeholder Constitutional law professors; Elder welfare law scholars; Technology law researchers; Legal aid lawyers specializing in elderly rights; Maintenance Tribunal practitioners Applicable Cities Delhi primarily; accessible from any city Session Duration 60–75 minutes Administration Mode Audio-recorded with consent. Opening: I would like to discuss the constitutional and legal dimensions of digital exclusion for the oldest old in India's Smart Cities. To begin -how do you interpret the scope of Article 21 (right to life with dignity and personal liberty) and Article 41 (right to public assistance) in relation to an oldest old person who is functionally unable to access state-provided public services because of mandatory digital gatekeeping? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 146. Is there existing jurisprudence -from the Supreme Court or High Courts -on the positive obligation of the state to ensure accessible delivery of public services for vulnerable or marginalised populations? 147. Can the doctrine of 'substantive dignity' under Article 21 be doctrinally extended to encompass access to digital public infrastructure? What would be the strongest constitutional argument for this position? 148. In your legal view, does ‘digital -by-default’ governance -where the state provides no viable non-digital alternative for a service -constitute a violation of an oldest old person's fundamental rights under Articles 14, 21, or 41? 149. How does the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act 2007 and the proposed 2019 Amendment Bill engage with or fail to engage with state-imposed digital barriers? What is the precise legislative gap? 150. What specific legislative intervention -whether an amendment, a new provision, or a new statute -would most effectively protect oldest old persons from digital exclusion in public spaces? 151. Is there any doctrine of indirect discrimination or disparate impact in Indian constitutional law that might be applied to age-based digital exclusion? 152. How do international frameworks -including the UNCRPD, the UN Principles for Older Persons, and WHO age-friendly cities guidance -inform Indian constitutional interpretation in this legal space? 153. What is your considered view on redefining ‘welfare’ and ‘dignity’ under Indian elder law to explicitly encompass digital autonomy and infrastructure equity? 154. Are there any pending cases or recent judgments -at any court level -that touch on these issues that we should be examining? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 38



