Data Collection Tools TOOL 9: FOCUS GROUP DISCUSSION GUIDE -OLDEST OLD PERSONS AND CAREGIVERS (FGD-OOP/CG-v1.0) Instrument Code FGD-OOP/CG-v1.0 Instrument Title Focus Group Discussion Guide -Oldest Old Persons and Primary Caregivers Type Qualitative -Facilitated Focus Group Discussion with Interactive Activities Target Stakeholder FGD-A: Oldest Old Persons only (6-8 participants). FGD-B: Primary Caregivers only (6-8 participants). NEVER mix both groups in a single FGD. Applicable Cities All six cities -minimum 2 FGDs per city (1 x OOP; 1 x Caregiver). Additional FGDs in Delhi and Chennai. Session Duration 90–120 minutes per FGD. Structured in 4 timed phases. Administration Mode Audio-recorded with group consent. One trained note-taker in addition to facilitator. City language. Administration Instructions: FGD TEAM ROLES AND LOGISTICS:[ ] Facilitator: guides discussion, manages group dynamics, uses probes. Does NOT take notes during discussion.[ ] Note-taker: records non-verbal responses, group dynamics, key verbatim quotes, body language, who is speaking/silent. Operates audio recorder.[ ] Venue: private room with chairs in a circle or around a table -no hierarchical seating arrangement.[ ] Refreshments provided BEFORE starting (builds rapport and comfort).[ ] Participants use only first names or chosen names -not full names.[ ] Separate FGDs for OOP and Caregivers are MANDATORY -never mix the two.[ ] Ensure minimum 40% women participants in each FGD.[ ] [Aizawl]: Coordinate FGD through church or YMA networks; begin with brief community prayer if participants wish.[ ] [Srinagar]: Female-only FGD option available; local community liaison must be present for access and safety. FGD Code FGD Type [ ] FGD-A (OOP) [ ] FGD-B (Caregiver) City / Venue Date and Time Facilitator Code Note-taker Code No. of Participants Total: ___ Male: ___ Female: ___ Transgender: ___ Audio File ID PHASE 1 OPENING, GROUND RULES, AND WARM-UP [15 minutes] Facilitator’s Opening Statement (read to the group after refreshments): Thank you all for coming today. We are researchers from [Institution] working on a study about how life in [city] has been changing for older people. We are here purely to listen and learn from you -we are not from the government and we are not here to judge anything. Everything shared today will be kept completely private and your names will never appear in any report. [Pause] Please speak freely. There are no right or wrong answers. Your real experiences are what matter to us. ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 47



Data Collection Tools Ground Rules (facilitator explains briefly): • One person speaks at a time -all voices are equally important and welcome. • There are no right or wrong answers. Your personal experience, however big or small, is exactly what we need. • What is shared in this room stays in this room -please respect everyone’s privacy. • You may pass on any question you prefer not to answer. • We will gently guide the conversation -please do not take it personally if we move on. Ice-Breaker Activity [10 minutes]: Each participant shares three things: (1) their first name, (2) how long they have lived in [city], and (3) one thing they personally enjoy doing in this city. Facilitator participates briefly to model ease and informality. Note-taker records which participants are more or less talkative for attention during the main discussion. PHASE 2 TRANSITION QUESTIONS -Introducing the Main Topic Gently [20 minutes] Transition Q1: Let us talk about daily life in [city] for people our age. When you need to do something ordinary -pay a bill, visit a doctor, take public transport, go to a government office, or visit a park -what does that actually involve these days? Who would like to start with something that happened recently? Field Investigator Note: Do NOT use the words digital, app, or smartphone yet in Phase 2. Let these concepts emerge organically from participants own narrations. This is a key analytical indicator -note in the post-FGD log whether participants raised digital barriers spontaneously or only after the facilitator introduced the concept. Transition Q2: In recent years, many services in the city have changed -some things are now done through mobile phones, websites, or machines at service points. Has anyone in the group noticed this? How has it specifically affected the way you go about your day? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 162. What specific services have you noticed have moved online or to apps or to digital machines? 163. Was there a particular moment when you first realized things had changed in a way that made life harder or more confusing for you? 164. Has anything become easier for you personally because of these changes, in fairness? PHASE 3 KEY DISCUSSION QUESTIONS [55 minutes -approximately 11 minutes per question] KQ1 -Barriers, Friction, and Exclusion: When you think about all the different things you need to do in this city -transport, health services, utilities, government offices, parks -which ones feel most difficult or impossible to access these days? Why do you think that is? ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 48



Data Collection Tools Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 165. Has anyone been told that a service is ‘only available online’ when you needed it physically? What happened next? 166. What do you do when you cannot access something alone -who do you turn to, and how does that process feel? 167. Has anyone paid a middleman, an agent, or a cyber cafe to do something that should have been free and direct? 168. [City-specific probe] 'What about [prominent SCM service in this city]? Has anyone in the group tried to use it? What happened?' KQ2 -Emotional Impact and Dignity: How do you feel -emotionally -when you are unable to do something on your own that your children or grandchildren can do easily on their phones? I know this is a personal question and you only need to share as much as you are comfortable with. Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 169. Has anyone felt publicly embarrassed or ashamed in a specific situation because of a digital barrier? 170. Does depending on others for these tasks affect how you see yourself or your sense of self-worth? 171. Has anyone stopped doing something -visiting a particular place, claiming a particular benefit -because the digital barrier made it too difficult or uncomfortable to face? KQ3 -Social Connections and Intergenerational Impact: Let us talk about family -your children, grandchildren, and other younger people in your life. Has the difference in how you use technology compared to them affected your relationship with them in any way? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 172. Are there people in your family or neighbourhood who help you with digital tasks? How do you feel about regularly asking for that help? 173. Do you ever feel that younger people are impatient or dismissive when you struggle with technology? Can someone share a specific experience? 174. [Aizawl-specific] In Mizoram, community bonds through the church and YMA are very strong. Does your community actively help older members with digital city services? 175. [Chennai-specific] Are there women’s groups or community organisations in your area that help older women navigate city services? KQ4 -Rights, Government Responsibility, and Policy: In your view -does the government, whether at the city level or the national level, have a responsibility to ensure that ALL citizens, including those who cannot use digital devices, can access public services? Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 176. Do you feel this city has fulfilled that responsibility toward people your age? 177. Have any of you ever made a formal complaint about being excluded from a service because of a digital barrier? What happened? 178. Are you aware of any rights or laws that protect older citizens from being treated this way? 179. Do you feel that the voices and needs of older people are genuinely heard when the city plans and builds these new systems? KQ5 -Solutions, Vision, and Recommendations: If you could change ONE thing about how this city provides services to make life genuinely better and easier for people your age, what would it be? Let us hear from everyone -even small suggestions are very valuable. ICSSR Major Research Project 2025–26 | Smart Cities, Silent Citizens | 49



Data Collection Tools Probing Questions / Follow-ups: 180. Should there always be a non-digital option for every service -a physical counter, a phone line, a visiting officer? What would that look like? 181. Should the city provide trained helpers at digital facilities specifically to assist older residents? 182. What would a truly ‘age-friendly Smart City’ look and feel like to you? PHASE 4 CLOSING ACTIVITY AND WRAP-UP [20 minutes] Closing Activity -Message Cards [10 minutes]: Distribute one large index card and a thick marker pen to each participant. Ask them to write -or dictate to the note-taker if writing is difficult -their completion of this sentence: ‘If I could send one message to the person who builds and runs city services, I would say…’ Cards are collected by the note-taker and photographed with consent. Content is thematically coded as a separate data source. Closing Round [5 minutes]: Each participant answers briefly: Before we finish -is there anything important we have not discussed, that you feel we should know about living as an older person in [city] today? [Facilitator closing:] Thank you so deeply for your time, your honesty, and your trust in sharing your experiences with us today. What you have shared will directly inform recommendations to improve city services for older people across India. We will share a summary of findings when the research is complete. If you have any questions or concerns, please speak with our team. Post-FGD Analytical Log (Facilitator and Note-taker complete jointly within 2 hours) Dominant Themes (Top 5)

